sition. Grytczuk, Kordulewski, and Niewiadomski proved that there\nexist inﬁnitely many ternary extremal square-free words. We establish that there are no\nextremal square-free words over any alphabet of size at least 17.\nKeywords. Combinatorics on words, square-free words, extremal words\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcations. 05A05, 05D10, 68R15\n1. Introduction\nA word is a ﬁnite sequence of letters over a ﬁnite alphabet. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNo extremal square-free words over large alphabets - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2107.13123v2)\nciteturn14search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## No extremal square-free words over large alphabets\n\nLetong Hong, Shengtong Zhang ... We establish that there are no extremal square-free words over any alphabet of size at least 17.\n\n## No extremal square-free words over large alphabets\n\nLetong Hong, Shengtong Zhang\n\nPublished: 2021-07-28 | Updated: 2021-09-09\n\nComments: 8 pages, no figures. Found a small mistake in Proposition 2.2 that slightly weakens our bound\n\nNote:\n\nJournal: Combin. Theory 2, 1 (2022) #17\n\nDOI: 10.5070/C62156889\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nA word is square-free if it does not contain any square (a word of the form $XX$), and is extremal square-free if it cannot be extended to a new square-free word by inserting a single letter at any position. Grytczuk, Kordulewski, and Niewiadomski proved that there exist infinitely many ternary extremal square-free words. We establish that there are no extremal square-free words over any alphabet of size at least 17.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSquare-Free Words: Theme and Variations (https://faculty.tru.ca/lmol/Slides/SquareFreeWordsCBU2022.pdf)\nciteturn14search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Q: Are there extremal square-free words over larger alphabets? ... ▶Theorem (Hong and Zhang 2022): There are no extremal\nsquare-free words over alphabets of size 17 or greater.\nFURTHER QUESTIONS\nQ: Are there extremal square-free words of every sufﬁciently\nlarge length?\nA: Yes.\n▶Theorem (Mol and Rampersad 2021): There is an\nextremal square-free word of length k over {a, b, c} for\nevery k ≥87.\n▶In fact, there are many! The number grows exponentially in\nk.\nQ: Are there extremal square-free words over larger alphabets?\nA: Hmmmm...\n▶Conjecture (GKN 2020): No.\n▶Theorem (Hong and Zhang 2022): There are no extremal\nsquare-free words over alphabets of size 17 or greater.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1 Introduction (https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL24/Grytczuk/grytczuk7.pdf)\nciteturn14search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; There exist infinitely many extremal squarefree words over a 3-letter alphabet. ... In particular, they proved that for every k ≥ 87 there exists an extremal ternary word of length k. ... Computational experiments prompted us to state the following conjecture.\nFor instance, the word bear is an extension of the word bar by inserting the letter e between letters b and a. A squarefree word W ∈ A* is maximal if for every x ∈ A the extensions xW and Wx contain a square. A squarefree word W is called extremal over A if there is no squarefree extension of W. For instance, the word\n\nH = 1231213231232123121323123\n\nis the shortest extremal word over the alphabet {1, 2, 3}. This means that inserting any letter from the alphabet {1, 2, 3} at any position in the word H, including the beginning as well as the end of H, results with a square.\n\nA natural intuition is that extremal words should be rare or even should have bounded length. However, in the case of a 3-letter alphabet, this intuition turned out to be faulty.\n\nTheorem 1 (Grytczuk, Kordulewski, and Niewiadomski [7]). There exist infinitely many extremal squarefree words over a 3-letter alphabet.\n\nThe proof of this theorem is by a recursive construction whose validity is partially based on computer verification. Mol and Rampersad [11] determined all positive integers k for which there exist extremal ternary words of length exactly k. In particular, they proved that for every k ≥ 87 there exists an extremal ternary word of length k.\n\nOne may naturally wonder what the case is for larger alphabets. Actually, we do not know if there are any extremal words over a 4-letter alphabet. Computational experiments prompted us to state the following conjecture.\n\nConjecture 2 (Grytczuk, Kordulewski, and Niewiadomski [7]). Every squarefree word over a 4-letter alphabet can be extended to a squarefree word.\n\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNo extremal square-free words over large alphabets — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/no-extremal-square-free-words-over-large-alphabets)\nciteturn14search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; This research paper, titled \"No extremal square-free words over large alphabets,\" by Letong Hong and Shengtong Zhang, tackles a long-standing question in the field of combinatorics on words. ...   * Theorem 1.3: For any alphabet size $k \\ge 17$k≥17, there exists no extremal square-free word.\n\n# No extremal square-free words over large alphabets\n\nLetong Hong, Shengtong Zhang\n\nComb. Theory 2022 3\n\nThis research paper, titled \"No extremal square-free words over large alphabets,\" by Letong Hong and Shengtong Zhang, tackles a long-standing question in the field of combinatorics on words. It provides a definitive proof that for sufficiently large alphabets, you can always find a way to extend a square-free word without creating a \"square.\"\n\n## TL;DR\n\nThe authors prove that for any alphabet with 17 or more letters, \"extremal\" square-free words do not exist. This means that in a large enough alphabet, you can never get \"stuck\" when trying to insert a new letter into a square-free word; there will always be at least one position and one letter that keeps the word square-free.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nIn combinatorics, a square is a word formed by repeating a sequence twice (e.g., \"hotshots\" is \"hotshot\" + \"hotshot\"). A word is square-free if it contains no such patterns.\n\nThe study of square-free words dates back to Axel Thue in 1906, who showed that you can create infinitely long square-free words using only three letters (a ternary alphabet). \n## Datasets\n\nNot discussed in the paper. This is a theoretical mathematics paper and does not rely on empirical datasets or machine learning benchmarks.\n\n## Key Insights and Ideas\n\n  * The \"Sign\" of an Insertion: The authors categorize insertions based on whether the new letter falls in the first or second half of the newly created square. This \"sign\" helps them prove that similar insertions must be spaced far apart, preventing a \"clustering\" of squares that could block many gaps at once.\n  * Linear Growth of Constraints: The most surprising insight is that the number of \"blocking\" insertions grows only linearly with the length of the word, and the constant factor (14.7) is relatively small.\n  * Nonchalant Words: The paper applies its main theorem to \"nonchalant words\"—words generated by a greedy algorithm that always picks the \"earliest\" possible letter and shortest possible extension. They prove that for $k \\ge 17$k≥17, this greedy process will never terminate and will instead converge to an infinite square-free word.\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * Theorem 1.3: For any alphabet size $k \\ge 17$k≥17, there exists no extremal square-free word.\n  * Corollary 1.4: For $k \\ge 17$k≥17, the sequence of nonchalant words over a fixed alphabet converges to an infinite word.\n  * Quantitative Bound: The authors established that the number of square-completing quadruples in a square-free word of length $n$n is less than $14.7n$14.7 n.\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\nThe primary limitation is the gap between the proven bound ($k=17$k=17) and the conjectured bound ($k=4$k=4). The authors acknowledge that their constant (14.7) is likely not optimal.\n\nFuture work will likely focus on refining the counting arguments or finding new structural properties of square-free words to lower the alphabet threshold from 17 down toward 4, potentially settling Conjecture 1.2 entirely. Additionally, the behavior of these words on alphabets of size 4 through 16 remains an open, intriguing mystery.\n\nBibTeX\n    \n    @article{hong2022no,\n      author = {Letong Hong and Shengtong Zhang},\n      title = {{No extremal square-free words over large alphabets}},\n      journal = {Comb. Theory},\n      year = {2022},\n      doi = {10.5070/c62156889},--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) No extremal square-free words over alphabets of size at least 5 (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/412165303_No_extremal_square-free_words_over_alphabets_of_size_at_least_5)\nciteturn14search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The following conjecture was posed in [3,6]. ... There is no extremal square-free word over an alphabet of size ... words over a linearly ordered alphabet (A,≺)is generated as follows. ... For any integer k≥17, there is no extremal square-free word over ... 1, Paper No.\n\nof size 3, it is conjectured that the sequence of nonchalant words is inﬁnite.\n\nConjecture 1.3 ([3,4]).For any alphabet A of size k≥3 with a linear ordering ≺, the\n\nsequence of nonchalant words over (A,≺)is inﬁnite.\n\nEven if the sequence of nonchalant words is inﬁnite, it still might not converge to an\n\ninﬁnite word. Therefore, the following conjecture is even stronger than the previous one.\n\nConjecture 1.4 ([3,4]).For any alphabet A of size k≥3 with a linear ordering ≺, the\n\nsequence of non